INT. SPARKLE-KING CAR WASH - NIGHT SHIFT - NIGHT

A drive-through tunnel gone quiet. Wet brushes hang motionless, dripping. A glass booth by the exit holds a space heater, a radio, and two plastic chairs. Fluorescent hum.

WREN LINDQVIST, 17, in a yellow company polo two sizes too big, sits on a chair with her feet up on a bucket. Beside her, DOTTIE ABERNATHY-REYES, 17, hair in a scarf, works a crossword with a pen chewed flat. Neither is looking at the other. It is 1:40 a.m.

The radio crackles. It is a handheld CB sitting on the console, a relic that the owner insists on.

HANK (O.S.)
(over radio)
Sparkle-King, this is Hank, you kids alive in there?

Wren picks it up and thumbs the button without sitting up.

WREN
Alive, Hank.

HANK (O.S.)
(over radio)
Good. Don't let anybody through the tunnel after two. The brush motor's acting up.

WREN
Nobody's coming through, Hank. It's a Tuesday.

HANK (O.S.)
(over radio)
That's how it gets you.

She sets the radio down. Dottie writes a word in her puzzle and erases it.

DOTTIE
Seven letters. "Feeling of wanting to be elsewhere."

WREN
Restless.

DOTTIE
That's eight.

WREN
Is it?

Dottie counts on her fingers, lips moving. Wren watches her do it, then looks away at the tunnel, at the brushes hanging still.

DOTTIE
You didn't apply to the program, did you.

WREN
Which program.

DOTTIE
The summer one. In Duluth. You said you would.

WREN
I said I might.

Dottie nods. She flips the paper, finds another clue, doesn't read it. She was supposed to be on the Duluth shuttle with Wren. She picks at the gummy edge of her pen cap.

DOTTIE
My mom thinks you're a bad influence.

WREN
I wash cars.

DOTTIE
She says that's what a bad influence would say.

They laugh, the small, unwilling laugh of two people who have heard the same joke for a year. The space heater ticks. Somewhere in the tunnel, a drip lands in a bucket, and then another.

WREN
Do you think they'll be mad? If I don't go?

DOTTIE
Who's they?

WREN
Everyone. Your mom. Mine. You.

DOTTIE
I'd be mad for like a week.

WREN
That's a long time.

DOTTIE
It's a week.

The radio crackles again.

HANK (O.S.)
(over radio)
Girls, I'm gonna need you to hit the emergency stop on the brush motor. I can hear it from the house.

Wren reaches for the radio. Dottie, without looking, hits the red button on the wall. The tunnel's low hum drops away and the silence is enormous.

HANK (O.S.) (CONT'D)
(over radio)
Thank you. Good work.

WREN
(to Dottie)
Good work.

DOTTIE
(to the crossword)
Good work.

Dottie reaches over and takes the pen out of her own mouth and offers it to Wren, who does not need it, and takes it anyway. They sit there in the quiet. Wren writes something in a square. Dottie watches it appear.

DOTTIE (CONT'D)
That's not seven letters.

WREN
It's what fits.

Dottie squints at the page. She reads it twice. She leans her head against Wren's shoulder, briefly, as if testing it, then straightens, as if she hadn't.

DOTTIE
Fine. It fits.

Outside the booth window, a single set of headlights swings off the road and slows toward the tunnel mouth. Neither of them sees it yet.

WREN
You want a pop?

DOTTIE
I want a lot of things.

WREN
(standing)
They've got orange.

DOTTIE
Orange is fine.

Wren walks to the machine in the corner. A coin drops. A can clatters down. She carries it back and cracks it open and hands it over and Dottie drinks, looking at the headlights, and then at her. The lights stop at the tunnel mouth and idle there.

WREN
(into the radio, tired)
Hank, we've got a car.

HANK (O.S.)
(over radio)
Tell 'em we're closed.

WREN
(to Dottie)
We're closed.

Dottie stands. Smooths her scarf. Walks to the booth door.

DOTTIE
I'll tell them.

She goes out. Wren watches her through the glass, a small figure walking toward the headlights, one hand lifted, shaking her head. The car's engine idles, then backs away. Dottie turns, walks back, and sits down.

DOTTIE (CONT'D)
They said thanks anyway.

WREN
For what?

DOTTIE
I don't know. Being closed.

Wren laughs, properly this time, and puts her feet back up on the bucket. The heater ticks. The radio is quiet. On the paper between them, a word sits in its squares, almost the right length.
